blocks.text:
&ldquo;Cain&rsquo;t tell for sartin; some thinks he&rsquo;s gwyne to be &rsquo;long toreckly, and
      some thinks &rsquo;e hain&rsquo;t. Russ Mosely he tote ole Hanks he mought git to
      Obeds tomorrer or nex&rsquo; day he reckoned.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I wisht I knowed. I got a &lsquo;prime sow and pigs in the cote-house,
      and I hain&rsquo;t got no place for to put &rsquo;em. If the jedge is a gwyne to hold
      cote, I got to roust &rsquo;em out, I reckon. But tomorrer&rsquo;ll do, I &rsquo;spect.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I believe I&rsquo;ll do it.&mdash;A man will just rot, here. My house my yard,
      everything around me, in fact, shows&rsquo; that I am becoming one of these
      cattle&mdash;and I used to be thrifty in other times.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Spoken like yourself, my child! But we&rsquo;ll not starve, Nancy. Far from it.
      I have a letter from Beriah Sellers&mdash;just came this day. A letter
      that&mdash;I&rsquo;ll read you a line from it!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Go! Well, I should think so, Nancy. It&rsquo;s all a chance, of course, and,
      chances haven&rsquo;t been kind to us, I&rsquo;ll admit&mdash;but whatever comes, old
      wife, they&rsquo;re provided for. Thank God for that!&rdquo;

&ldquo;You got to camp in my house as long as you hang out here,&rdquo; said one. &ldquo;If
      tha hain&rsquo;t room for you and yourn my tribe&rsquo;ll turn out and camp in the hay
      loft.&rdquo;

&ldquo;O Lord&rsquo;, we&rsquo;s ben mighty wicked, an&rsquo; we knows dat we &rsquo;zerve to go to de
      bad place, but good Lord, deah Lord, we ain&rsquo;t ready yit, we ain&rsquo;t ready&mdash;let
      dese po&rsquo; chilen hab one mo&rsquo; chance, jes&rsquo; one mo&rsquo; chance. Take de ole
      niggah if you&rsquo;s got to hab somebody.&mdash;Good Lord, good deah Lord, we
      don&rsquo;t know whah you&rsquo;s a gwyne to, we don&rsquo;t know who you&rsquo;s got yo&rsquo; eye on,
      but we knows by de way you&rsquo;s a comin&rsquo;, we knows by de way you&rsquo;s a tiltin&rsquo;
      along in yo&rsquo; charyot o&rsquo; fiah dat some po&rsquo; sinner&rsquo;s a gwyne to ketch it.
      But good Lord, dose chilen don&rsquo;t b&rsquo;long heah, dey&rsquo;s f&rsquo;m Obedstown whah dey
      don&rsquo;t know nuffin, an&rsquo; you knows, yo&rsquo; own sef, dat dey ain&rsquo;t &rsquo;sponsible.
      An&rsquo; deah Lord, good Lord, it ain&rsquo;t like yo&rsquo; mercy, it ain&rsquo;t like yo&rsquo; pity,
      it ain&rsquo;t like yo&rsquo; long-sufferin&rsquo; lovin&rsquo; kindness for to take dis kind o&rsquo;
      &rsquo;vantage o&rsquo; sick little chil&rsquo;en as dose is when dey&rsquo;s so many ornery grown
      folks chuck full o&rsquo; cussedness dat wants roastin&rsquo; down dah. Oh, Lord, spah
      de little chil&rsquo;en, don&rsquo;t tar de little chil&rsquo;en away f&rsquo;m dey frens, jes&rsquo;
      let &rsquo;em off jes&rsquo; dis once, and take it out&rsquo;n de ole niggah. HEAH I IS,
      LORD, HEAH I IS! De ole niggah&rsquo;s ready, Lord, de ole&mdash;&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;H&rsquo;wsh! Well now dey&rsquo;s some folks says dey ain&rsquo;t no &rsquo;ficiency in prah. Dis
      Chile would like to know whah we&rsquo;d a ben now if it warn&rsquo;t fo&rsquo; dat prah?
      Dat&rsquo;s it. Dat&rsquo;s it!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Does I reckon? Don&rsquo;t I know it! Whah was yo&rsquo; eyes? Warn&rsquo;t de Lord jes&rsquo; a
      cumin&rsquo; chow! chow! CHOW! an&rsquo; a goin&rsquo; on turrible&mdash;an&rsquo; do de Lord
      carry on dat way &rsquo;dout dey&rsquo;s sumfin don&rsquo;t suit him? An&rsquo; warn&rsquo;t he a
      lookin&rsquo; right at dis gang heah, an&rsquo; warn&rsquo;t he jes&rsquo; a reachin&rsquo; for &rsquo;em? An&rsquo;
      d&rsquo;you spec&rsquo; he gwyne to let &rsquo;em off &rsquo;dout somebody ast him to do it? No
      indeedy!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now mars Clay, you knows bettern dat. Sometimes a body can&rsquo;t tell whedder
      you&rsquo;s a sayin&rsquo; what you means or whedder you&rsquo;s a sayin&rsquo; what you don&rsquo;t
      mean, &rsquo;case you says &rsquo;em bofe de same way.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, I don&rsquo;t want the lead,&rdquo; said the pilot, &ldquo;I want you. Roust out the
      old man&mdash;tell him the Amaranth&rsquo;s coming. And go and call Jim&mdash;tell
      him.&rdquo;

A riot of shouting and trampling floated up from below, presently, and the
      uneasy steering of the boat soon showed that she was getting &ldquo;down by the
      head.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Jim,&rdquo; said George, looking straight ahead, watching the slightest yawing
      of the boat and promptly meeting it with the wheel, &ldquo;how&rsquo;ll it do to try
      Murderer&rsquo;s Chute?&rdquo;

&ldquo;A hundred and ninety-six and still a-swelling!&mdash;water, below the
      middle gauge-cocks!&mdash;carrying every pound she can stand!&mdash;nigger
      roosting on the safety-valve!&rdquo;

A jury of inquest was impaneled, and after due deliberation and inquiry
      they returned the inevitable American verdict which has been so familiar
      to our ears all the days of our lives&mdash;&ldquo;NOBODY TO BLAME.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Not for anything in the world. I love them just the same as I love my
      own: They pet me and spoil me even more than the others do, I think. I
      reckon we&rsquo;ll get along, Si.&rdquo;

&ldquo;The same old chap!&mdash;always just a little bit divided about things.&mdash;And
      what will you do when you get to be one of the richest men in the world,
      Clay?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Hang the fellow, I do believe he has gone wilder than ever, but still a
      body can&rsquo;t help liking him if they would&mdash;and what is more, they
      don&rsquo;t ever want to try when they see his eyes and hear him talk.&rdquo;

His title of &ldquo;Squire&rdquo; came into vogue again, but only for a season; for,
      as his wealth and popularity augmented, that title, by imperceptible
      stages, grew up into &ldquo;Judge;&rdquo; indeed it bade fair to swell into &ldquo;General&rdquo;
       bye and bye. All strangers of consequence who visited the village
      gravitated to the Hawkins Mansion and became guests of the &ldquo;Judge.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Si, I do not know what we are going to do. The children are not fit to be
      seen, their clothes are in such a state. But there&rsquo;s something more
      serious still.&mdash;There is scarcely a bite in the house to eat.&rdquo;

Hawkins&rsquo;s heart bounded within him. His whole frame was racked and
      wrenched with fettered hurrahs. His first impulse was to shout &ldquo;Done! and
      God bless the iron company, too!&rdquo;

&ldquo;If Col. Sellers would come for me, I would go and stay with him a while,
      till the Tennessee land is sold. He has often wanted me to come, ever
      since he moved to Hawkeye.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Father, they must know you there, and no doubt they would credit you in a
      moment, for a little stage ride like that. Couldn&rsquo;t you write and ask
      them?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Couldn&rsquo;t you, Washington&mdash;seeing it&rsquo;s you that wants the ride? And
      what do you think you&rsquo;ll do, Washington, when you get to Hawkeye? Finish
      your invention for making window-glass opaque?&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, sir. I believe I have found out the stuff that will do it, but it
      kills the hen; so I have dropped that for the present, though I can take
      it up again some day when I learn how to manage the mixture better.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, sir, three or four things. I think they are all good and can all be
      done, but they are tiresome, and besides they require money. But as soon
      as the land is sold&mdash;&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;I will go to St. Louis, too, sir. I will find a way to get there. I will
      make a way. And I will find a way to help myself along, and do what I can
      to help the rest, too.&rdquo;

&ldquo;So one of my girls is going to turn out and work for her living! It&rsquo;s
      like your pluck and spirit, child, but we will hope that we haven&rsquo;t got
      quite down to that, yet.&rdquo;

&ldquo;&mdash;&mdash;1,000,000 bottles in the United States&mdash;profit at
      least $350,000&mdash;and then it would begin to be time to turn our
      attention toward the real idea of the business.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You are my mother, and always shall be. We will be as we have always
      been; and neither this foolish talk nor any other thing shall part us or
      make us less to each other than we are this hour.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t believe it,&rdquo; said Philip, stoutly, &ldquo;its wrong in principle, and
      it ought not to succeed, but I don&rsquo;t see how I can go for a thing I don&rsquo;t
      believe in.&rdquo;

&ldquo;O, very well,&rdquo; said Gringo, turning away with a shade of contempt,
      &ldquo;you&rsquo;ll find if you are going into literature and newspaper work that you
      can&rsquo;t afford a conscience like that.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, it&rsquo;s a big operation. We are going, a lot of us, railroad men,
      engineers, contractors. You know my uncle is a great railroad man. I&rsquo;ve no
      doubt I can get you a chance to go if you&rsquo;ll go.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Field engineer, civil engineer. You can begin by carrying a rod, and
      putting down the figures. It&rsquo;s easy enough. I&rsquo;ll show you about that.
      We&rsquo;ll get Trautwine and some of those books.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why don&rsquo;t you see? We lay out a line, spot the good land, enter it up,
      know where the stations are to be, spot them, buy lots; there&rsquo;s heaps of
      money in it. We wouldn&rsquo;t engineer long.&rdquo;

&ldquo;He is in the saloon, playing poker with Schaick and that long haired
      party with the striped trousers, who scrambled aboard when the stage plank
      was half hauled in, and the big Delegate to Congress from out west.&rdquo;

&ldquo;That&rsquo;s a fine looking fellow, that delegate, with his glossy, black
      whiskers; looks like a Washington man; I shouldn&rsquo;t think he&rsquo;d be at
      poker.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You see,&rdquo; he says to Philip one morning at the hotel when he was thus
      engaged, &ldquo;I want to get the theory of this thing, so that I can have a
      check on the engineers.&rdquo;

In shaping their reply in their minds, the title &ldquo;Mr.&rdquo; had a place in it;
      but when their turn had arrived to speak, the title &ldquo;Colonel&rdquo; came from
      their lips instead.

&ldquo;Well, partly, sir, partly. I&rsquo;m down here now with reference to a little
      operation&mdash;a little side thing merely. By the way gentlemen, excuse
      the liberty, but it&rsquo;s about my usual time&rdquo;&mdash;

&ldquo;I always smoke this sort, gentlemen; they are a little more expensive,
      but you&rsquo;ll learn, in this climate, that you&rsquo;d better not economize on poor
      cigars.&rdquo;

&ldquo;By George, that&rsquo;s annoying. By George, that&rsquo;s mortifying. Never had
      anything of that kind happen to me before. I&rsquo;ve left my pocket-book. Hold!
      Here&rsquo;s a bill, after all. No, thunder, it&rsquo;s a receipt.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I know,&rdquo; said Margaret Bolton, with a half anxious smile, &ldquo;thee chafes
      against all the ways of Friends, but what will thee do? Why is thee so
      discontented?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Thy ways greatly try me, Ruth, and all thy relations. I desire thy
      happiness first of all, but thee is starting out on a dangerous path. Is
      thy father willing thee should go away to a school of the world&rsquo;s people?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Thee, study medicine! A slight frail girl like thee, study medicine! Does
      thee think thee could stand it six months? And the lectures, and the
      dissecting rooms, has thee thought of the dissecting rooms?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I hope at least, Ruth, thee will let us know when thee opens an office,&rdquo;
       said her mother, with an approach to sarcasm that she rarely indulged in,
      as she rose and left the room.

&ldquo;He doesn&rsquo;t say, but it&rsquo;s on the frontier, and on the map everything
      beyond it is marked &lsquo;Indians&rsquo; and &lsquo;desert,&rsquo; and looks as desolate as a
      Wednesday Meeting.&rdquo;

&ldquo;He doesn&rsquo;t say exactly what it is,&rdquo; said Ruth a little dubiously, &ldquo;but
      it&rsquo;s something about land and railroads, and thee knows, father, that
      fortunes are made nobody knows exactly how, in a new country.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Somewhat that depends upon whether her children amount to anything,&rdquo;
       retorted the sharp little disputant. &ldquo;What&rsquo;s the good, father, of a series
      of human beings who don&rsquo;t advance any?&rdquo;

&ldquo;But,&rdquo; said Margaret, &ldquo;consider her total inexperience of the world, and
      her frail health. Can such a slight little body endure the ordeal of the
      preparation for, or the strain of, the practice of the profession?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, what would become of the poor people who had been led to put their
      little money into the speculation, when you got out of it and left it half
      way?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Father, I think thee&rsquo;d better have his ill-will than his company. Is it
      true that he gave money to help build the pretty little church of St.
      James the Less, and that he is, one of the vestrymen?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I&rsquo;m to have, with another party,&rdquo; said Harry, &ldquo;a big contract in the
      road, as soon as it is let; and, meantime, I&rsquo;m with the engineers to spy
      out the best land and the depot sites.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, I haven&rsquo;t got much in ready money I can lay my hands on now, but if a
      fellow could do anything with fifteen or twenty thousand dollars, as a
      beginning, I shall draw for that when I see the right opening.&rdquo;

&ldquo;This, gentlemen,&rdquo; said Jeff, &ldquo;is Columbus River, alias Goose Run. If it
      was widened, and deepened, and straightened, and made, long enough, it
      would be one of the finest rivers in the western country.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You&rsquo;ll have to negotiate with the contractors about the rails, sir,&rdquo; said
      Jeff; &ldquo;here&rsquo;s Mr. Brierly, I&rsquo;ve no doubt would like to buy your rails when
      the time comes.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well,&rdquo; said Mr. Thompson, rising from his seat and moving towards his
      tent, &ldquo;the railroad has come to Stone&rsquo;s Landing, sure; I move we take a
      drink on it all round.&rdquo;

The next morning the camp moved on, followed till it was out of sight by
      the listless eyes of the group in front of the store, one of whom remarked
      that, &ldquo;he&rsquo;d be doggoned if he ever expected to see that railroad any mo&rsquo;.&rdquo;

&ldquo;O bother what I said,&rdquo;&mdash;and the Colonel took up his sword to buckle
      it on, and then continued coolly, &ldquo;the fact is Laura, our romance is
      played out.&rdquo;

Laura heard, but she did not comprehend. She caught his arm and cried,
      &ldquo;George, how can you joke so cruelly? I will go any where with you. I will
      wait any where. I can&rsquo;t go back to Hawkeye.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It means,&rdquo; said the officer, in measured words, &ldquo;that you haven&rsquo;t
      anything to show for a legal marriage, and that I am going to New
      Orleans.&rdquo;

&ldquo;If a woman doesn&rsquo;t know, it&rsquo;s useless to tell her. What do you suppose I
      am staying in Hawkeye for, week after week, when I ought to be with my
      corps?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Harry!&rdquo; exclaimed Laura, touching his arm and letting her pretty hand
      rest there a moment. &ldquo;Why should I want you to go away? The only person in
      Hawkeye who understands me.&rdquo;

And when he turned away she followed him with a look that might have
      disturbed his serenity, if he had not at the moment had a little square
      letter in his breast pocket, dated at Philadelphia, and signed &ldquo;Ruth.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Providence,&rdquo; he said, &ldquo;has placed them in our hands, and although you and
      I, General, might have chosen a different destiny for them, under the
      Constitution, yet Providence knows best.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You educate the niggro and you make him more speculating than he was
      before. If he won&rsquo;t stick to any industry except for himself now, what
      will he do then?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Never, sir, never. He would only have a wider scope to injure himself. A
      niggro has no grasp, sir. Now, a white man can conceive great operations,
      and carry them out; a niggro can&rsquo;t.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I&rsquo;d elevate his soul,&rdquo; promptly responded the Colonel; &ldquo;that&rsquo;s just it;
      you can&rsquo;t make his soul too immortal, but I wouldn&rsquo;t touch him, himself.
      Yes, sir! make his soul immortal, but don&rsquo;t disturb the niggro as he is.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I see,&rdquo; nodded the Senator. &ldquo;But you&rsquo;d better begin by asking only for
      two or three hundred thousand, the usual way. You can begin to sell town
      lots on that appropriation you know.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You&rsquo;ve a pleasant town here, sir, and the most comfortable looking hotel
      I&rsquo;ve seen out of New York,&rdquo; said Harry to the clerk; &ldquo;we shall stay here a
      few days if you can give us a roomy suite of apartments.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It&rsquo;s a long time, though, since you have been here to say so,&rdquo; Alice
      said, with her father&rsquo;s frankness of manner; &ldquo;and I suspect we owe the
      visit now to your sudden interest in the Fallkill Seminary.&rdquo;

&ldquo;That accounts for Phil&rsquo;s wish to build a Seminary at Stone&rsquo;s Landing, our
      place in Missouri, when Col. Sellers insisted it should be a University.
      Phil appears to have a weakness for Seminaries.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, you&rsquo;ll see. When Sellers and I get that appropriation, I&rsquo;ll show
      you an establishment in town and another on the Hudson and a box at the
      opera.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Ruth is a dear girl, Philip, and has as much firmness of purpose as ever,
      but don&rsquo;t you see she has just discovered that she is fond of society?
      Don&rsquo;t you let her see you are selfish about it, is my advice.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It is beautiful to hear him talk when his heart is full of thankfulness
      for some manifestation of the Divine favor. You shall know him, some day
      my Louise, and knowing him you will honor him, as I do.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, I know,&rdquo; said the Colonel; &ldquo;I know they can&rsquo;t wait for the orders to
      go to New York and be cashed, but what&rsquo;s the reason they can&rsquo;t get them
      discounted in Hawkeye?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Perhaps thee doesn&rsquo;t know thee own mind; I have known persons of thy age
      who did not. Did thee see anybody whom thee would like to live with always
      in Fallkill?&rdquo;

Margaret Bolton smiled at this new-fangled philosophy. &ldquo;Thee will find
      that love, Ruth, is a thing thee won&rsquo;t reason about, when it comes, nor
      make any bargains about. Thee wrote that Philip Sterling was at Fallkill.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes. Father advanced money and took land as security, and whatever has
      gone with the money and the bonds, he has on his hands a large tract of
      wild land.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It has good timber, if it could ever be got out, and father says that
      there must be coal in it; it&rsquo;s in a coal region. He wants Philip to survey
      it, and examine it for indications of coal.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No. But tell me father,&rdquo; continued Ruth, not to be put off, &ldquo;is thee
      still going on with that Bigler and those other men who come here and
      entice thee?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Ah, father, thee sees every thing in a rose-colored light. I do believe
      thee wouldn&rsquo;t have so readily allowed me to begin the study of medicine,
      if it hadn&rsquo;t had the novelty of an experiment to thee.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It&rsquo;s all right, my dear, all right; it will all come right in a little
      while. There&rsquo;s $200,000 coming, and that will set things booming again:
      Harry seems to be having some difficulty, but that&rsquo;s to be expected&mdash;you
      can&rsquo;t move these big operations to the tune of Fisher&rsquo;s Hornpipe, you
      know. But Harry will get it started along presently, and then you&rsquo;ll see!
      I expect the news every day now.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, yes; yes&mdash;I don&rsquo;t know but I have. But anyway, the longer it&rsquo;s
      delayed, the nearer it grows to the time when it will start&mdash;same as
      every day you live brings you nearer to&mdash;nearer&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;We perceive by your reports and the reports of the Chief Superintendent,
      that you have been making gratifying progress with the work.&mdash;We are
      all very much pleased.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, my dear Mr. Brierly, there must be some mistake, I am sure we wrote
      you and also Mr. Sellers, recently&mdash;when my clerk comes he will show
      copies&mdash;letters informing you of the ten per cent. assessment.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Of course&mdash;but I didn&rsquo;t know that $200,000 was so very paltry.
      Though I grant, of course, that it is not a large sum, strictly speaking.
      But where is it?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well it does seem to alter the complexion of things a little. You are
      acquainted with the members down there, of course, else you could not have
      worked to such advantage?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I know them all, sir. I know their wives, their children, their babies&mdash;I
      even made it a point to be on good terms with their lackeys. I know every
      Congressman well&mdash;even familiarly.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why I know their handwriting as well as I know my own&mdash;have had
      correspondence enough with them, I should think. And their signatures&mdash;why
      I can tell their initials, even.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now here, for instance; do you believe that that is a genuine letter? Do
      you know this signature here?&mdash;and this one? Do you know who those
      initials represent&mdash;and are they forgeries?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Of course I ought to have suspected him, but I don&rsquo;t believe it ever
      really occurred to me. Well, well, well&mdash;how did you ever have the
      nerve to approach him, of all others?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Come, now, Mr. President, that&rsquo;s plenty of that! I take back everything I
      said on that head. I&rsquo;m a wiser man to-day than I was yesterday, I can tell
      you.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, of course. I understand the necessity of that. I will not betray the
      names. But to go back a bit, it begins to look as if you never saw any of
      that appropriation at all?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Which leaves us in debt some $25,000 at this moment. Salaries of home
      officers are still going on; also printing and advertising. Next month
      will show a state of things!&rdquo;

&ldquo;All these people are in the next Congress. We shan&rsquo;t have to pay them a
      cent. And what is more, they will work like beavers for us&mdash;perhaps
      it might be to their advantage.&rdquo;

&ldquo;We send many missionaries to lift up the benighted races of other lands.
      How much cheaper and better it would be if those people could only come
      here and drink of our civilization at its fountain head.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I perfectly agree with you, Mr. Beverly. Must you go? Well, good morning.
      Look in, when you are passing; and whenever I can give you any information
      about our affairs and prospects, I shall be glad to do it.&rdquo;

He recovered himself, reached the bell rope, &ldquo;Damn you, I&rsquo;ll learn you,&rdquo;
       stepped to the door and called a couple of brakemen, and then, as the
      speed slackened; roared out,

&ldquo;Why, Phil,&rdquo; she would say, &ldquo;what puts you in the dumps to day? You are as
      solemn as the upper bench in Meeting. I shall have to call Alice to raise
      your spirits; my presence seems to depress you.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It&rsquo;s not your presence, but your absence when you are present,&rdquo; began
      Philip, dolefully, with the idea that he was saying a rather deep thing.
      &ldquo;But you won&rsquo;t understand me.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It might be more cheerful than you are at times,&rdquo; Ruth replied with a
      laugh. &ldquo;But you mustn&rsquo;t do it without consulting Alice. She might not like
      it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Bless you, no. It never entered my head. Are you? The thought of Philip
      Sterling in love is too comical. I thought you were only in love with the
      Ilium coal mine, which you and father talk about half the time.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I can tell you one thing, Philip,&rdquo; she said, &ldquo;if ever Ruth Bolton loves,
      it will be with her whole soul, in a depth of passion that will sweep
      everything before it and surprise even herself.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You must have,&rdquo; said Philip. &ldquo;Was that where you learned to play the
      bones? He is a master of those musical instruments, Ruth; he plays well
      enough to go on the stage.&rdquo;

Harry said, &ldquo;I have it in mind. I believe I would begin attending lectures
      this winter if it weren&rsquo;t for being wanted in Washington. But medicine is
      particularly women&rsquo;s province.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, the treatment of disease is a good deal a matter of sympathy. A
      woman&rsquo;s intuition is better than a man&rsquo;s. Nobody knows anything, really,
      you know, and a woman can guess a good deal nearer than a man.&rdquo;

The tumult was over in a minute, and the next, laughter was heard, and not
      a few said, &ldquo;I knew it wasn&rsquo;t anything.&rdquo; &ldquo;What fools people are at such a
      time.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Indeed they won&rsquo;t. You&rsquo;ll see. There will never be a woman in Washington
      that can compare with you. You&rsquo;ll be famous within a fortnight, Laura.
      Everybody will want to know you. You wait&mdash;you&rsquo;ll see.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It is its nature of old, here,&rdquo; said the daughter&mdash;stating it
      apparently as a fact, only, and by her manner waving aside all personal
      responsibility on account of it. &ldquo;Is it not so, mamma?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, I think perhaps it did. He had a great respect for Nature. He often
      said there was something striking about the ocean. You remember his saying
      that, daughter?&rdquo;

&ldquo;And hurricanes... He took a great interest in hurricanes. And animals.
      Dogs, especially&mdash;hunting dogs. Also comets. I think we all have our
      predilections. I think it is this that gives variety to our tastes.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I do find it depressing sometimes, but then there is so much about me
      here that is novel and interesting that my days are made up more of
      sunshine than shadow.&rdquo;

&ldquo;We of Washington are unfortunately situated in that respect,&rdquo; said the
      dowager. &ldquo;It is a tedious distance to Newport. But there is no help for
      it.&rdquo;

Laura said to herself, &ldquo;Long Branch and Cape May are nearer than Newport;
      doubtless these places are low; I&rsquo;ll feel my way a little and see.&rdquo; Then
      she said aloud:

&ldquo;Newport is damp, and cold, and windy and excessively disagreeable,&rdquo; said
      the daughter, &ldquo;but it is very select. One cannot be fastidious about minor
      matters when one has no choice.&rdquo;

Laura&mdash;&ldquo;The old families seem to be holding themselves aloof, from
      what I hear. I suppose you seldom meet in society now, the people you used
      to be familiar with twelve or fifteen years ago?&rdquo;

&ldquo;In the committee of the whole things are working very well. We have made
      ever so much progress in a week. I believe that you and I together could
      run this government beautifully, uncle.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Eh? he is one of my friends, Laura. He&rsquo;s a fine man, a very fine man. I
      don&rsquo;t know any man in congress I&rsquo;d sooner go to for help in any Christian
      work. What did he say?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Not shy, child, cautious. He&rsquo;s a very cautious man. I have been with him
      a great deal on conference committees. He wants reasons, good ones. Didn&rsquo;t
      you show him he was in error about the bill?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I hope so; his name will give tone and strength to it. I knew you would
      only have to show him that it was just and pure, in order to secure his
      cordial support.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I explained it to him. He said it was all right, he only wanted a word
      with you,&rdquo;, continued Laura. &ldquo;He is a handsome old gentleman, and he is
      gallant for an old man.&rdquo;

&ldquo;There, there, child. I meant nothing, Balloon talks a little freely
      sometimes, with men. But he is right at heart. His term expires next year
      and I fear we shall lose him.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, yes, but child, all Congressmen do that. It may not be strictly
      honest, indeed it is not unless he had some public documents mixed in with
      the clothes.&rdquo;

&ldquo;John Jay and Benjamin Franklin were well enough in their day, but the
      nation has made progress since then. Balloon is a man we know and can
      depend on to be true to himself.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, and Balloon has had a good deal of public experience. He is an old
      friend of mine. He was governor of one of the territories a while, and was
      very satisfactory.&rdquo;

&ldquo;But you didn&rsquo;t ask me, ma&rsquo;m. We always help customers all we can. You see
      our experience&mdash;living right among books all the time&mdash;that sort
      of thing makes us able to help a customer make a selection, you know.&rdquo;

&ldquo;O yes,&rdquo; said the clerk, a little perplexed as to whether to feel
      comfortable or the reverse; &ldquo;Oh yes, indeed, I&rsquo;ve often noticed that,
      ma&rsquo;m.&rdquo;

It was about the hour, now, for the chairman of the House Committee on
      Benevolent Appropriations to make his appearance, and Laura stepped to the
      door to reconnoiter. She glanced up the street, and sure enough&mdash;

&ldquo;Oh, yes&mdash;that is, it may be and it may not be. If you have known
      what perfection is in woman, it is fair to argue that inferiority cannot
      interest you now.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I wish in all sincerity that I could be worthy of such a felicitous
      compliment as that. But I am a woman, and so I am gratified for it just as
      it is, and would not have it altered.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Indeed it was no trouble. It was a pleasure. I am alone in the world
      since I lost my wife, and I often long for the society of your sex, Miss
      Hawkins, notwithstanding what people may say to the contrary.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Indeed you would always find a hearty welcome, Mr. Buckstone. I have
      often wished you would come and tell me more about Cairo and the Pyramids,
      as you once promised me you would.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, they are not so fickle as gentlemen&rsquo;s promises. And besides, if I had
      been inclined to forget, I&mdash;did you not give me something by way of a
      remembrancer?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Give it to me, Mr. Buckstone, and do not speak so. I have been
      sufficiently punished for my thoughtlessness. You cannot take pleasure in
      adding to my distress. Please give it to me.&rdquo;

&ldquo;If I were sure that you&mdash;&rdquo; She threw the spray away, and continued:
      &ldquo;This is silly! We will change the subject. No, do not insist&mdash;I must
      have my way in this.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You don&rsquo;t say so? I thought he was some guy from Pennsylvania. But he is
      different from others. Probably he has lived all his life on his
      plantation.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, yes, aren&rsquo;t they queer. But so high-bred, so picturesque. Do you
      think that color makes any difference, Mr. Hawkins? I used to be so
      prejudiced against color.&rdquo;

&ldquo;He wants to make himself conspicuous more like,&rdquo; said Laura. &ldquo;He always,
      you have noticed, talks with one eye on the gallery, while the other is on
      the speaker.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, my husband says, its nonsense to talk of war, and wicked. He knows
      what war is. If we do have war, I hope it will be for the patriots of
      Cuba. Don&rsquo;t you think we want Cuba, Mr. Hawkins?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I think we want it bad,&rdquo; said Washington. &ldquo;And Santo Domingo. Senator
      Dilworthy says, we are bound to extend our religion over the isles of the
      sea. We&rsquo;ve got to round out our territory, and&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;It&rsquo;s pleasant to hear you say so, Miss Hawkins. I believe my friends like
      to come here. Though society in Washington is mixed; we have a little of
      everything.&rdquo;

That afternoon, Laura wrote and dispatched the following note. It was in a
      round hand, unlike her flowing style, and it was directed to a number and
      street in Georgetown:&mdash;

&ldquo;Laura, stop! think! Suppose I loved you yet! Suppose I hated my fate!
      What can I do? I am broken by the war. I have lost everything almost. I
      had as lief be dead and done with it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Damn the woman,&rdquo; said the Colonel as he picked his way down the steps.
      &ldquo;Or,&rdquo; he added, as his thoughts took a new turn, &ldquo;I wish my wife was in
      New Orleans.&rdquo;

&ldquo;We must create a public opinion,&rdquo; said Senator Dilworthy. &ldquo;My only
      interest in it is a public one, and if the country wants the institution,
      Congress will have to yield.&rdquo;

&ldquo;If they will not lease,&rdquo; said Washington, &ldquo;let &rsquo;em make it two millions
      for an undivided half. I&rsquo;m not going to throw it away, not the whole of
      it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, that&rsquo;s all right,&rdquo; said Harry, &ldquo;if it&rsquo;s business. But if a woman
      looked at me as I&rsquo;ve seen her at Selby, I should understand it. And it&rsquo;s
      talked about, I can tell you.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I don&rsquo;t believe anything against you, Laura, but Col. Selby does not mean
      you any good. I know you wouldn&rsquo;t be seen with him if you knew his
      reputation.&rdquo;

Laura read the letter, with a little sigh may be, as she thought of other
      days, but with contempt also, and she put it into the fire with the
      thought, &ldquo;They are all alike.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I see now. I can manage it, I am sure. Indeed I wonder he never thought
      of it himself&mdash;there are no end of precedents. But how is this going
      to benefit you, after I have managed it? There is where the mystery lies.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I only wish I could see how; it is the oddest freak. You seem to go the
      furthest around to get at a thing&mdash;but you are in earnest, aren&rsquo;t
      you?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Vote for a fr&mdash;for a measure which is generally believed to be at
      least questionable? I am afraid we cannot come to an understanding, Miss
      Hawkins.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, not entirely so, perhaps. I and a person whom we will call Miss
      Blank (never mind the real name,) were in a closet at your elbow all the
      while.&rdquo;

&ldquo;If you had approached him, for instance, fortified with the fact that
      some of the best men in Congress, and the purest, etc., etc.; what would
      have been the result?&rdquo;

Mr. Trollop turned immediately back from the threshold. It might have been
      gladness that flashed into his face; it might have been something else;
      but at any rate there was much astonishment mixed with it.

&ldquo;Miss Hawkins, this silly blunder of my copyist evidently has more
      interest for you than it has for me. I will send my private secretary to
      you and let him discuss the subject with you at length.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Of course he did. Why all these questions? Tell me&mdash;how did you get
      hold of that page of manuscript? That is the only thing that stirs a
      passing interest in my mind.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, the thing has occurred before now. I mean that I have heard that
      Congressmen have sometimes hired literary grubs to build speeches for
      them.&mdash;Now didn&rsquo;t I overhear a conversation like that I spoke of?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well if it was only a joke, why did you make a serious matter of it? Why
      did you get the speech written for you, and then read it in the House
      without ever having it copied?&rdquo;

Mr. Trollop did not spring to his feet and smite his brow with his hand
      while a cold sweat broke out all over him and the color forsook his face&mdash;no,
      he only said, &ldquo;Good God!&rdquo; and looked greatly astonished.

&ldquo;Well, the wonderful tragedy is done, and it transpires that I am indebted
      to you for my late eloquence. What of it? What was all this for and what
      does it amount to after all? What do you propose to do about it?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well I had not thought of that. I only kept back the page for the mere
      fun of the thing; but since you mention it, I don&rsquo;t know but I might do
      something if I were angry.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, I feel more tenderly toward the oppressed colored man than I did.
      Shall we bury the hatchet and be good friends and respect each other&rsquo;s
      little secrets, on condition that I vote Aye on the measure?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I value your vote, Mr. Trollop, but I value your influence more. You are
      able to help a measure along in many ways, if you choose. I want to ask
      you to work for the bill as well as vote for it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;The child is born. Buckstone gives notice of the thieving Knobs
      University job. It is said the noses have been counted and enough votes
      have been bought to pass it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, uncle, dear; if your theory is right, let us go into raptures, for
      nobody can ask a heartier persecution than these editorials are
      furnishing.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Know all about him. Fine fellow. But he&rsquo;s got a wife; and I told him, as
      a friend, he&rsquo;d better sheer off from Laura. I reckon he thought better of
      it and did.&rdquo;

Mr. Clawson&mdash;&ldquo;Mr. Chairman, I move the insertion of the words five
      and twenty cents, as representing the true value of this barren and
      isolated tract of desolation.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Undoubtedly, undoubtedly. I have had their interests greatly at heart.
      There will of course be a few expenses, but the widow and orphans will
      realize all that Mr. Hawkins, dreamed of for them.&rdquo;

The occasion did not pass without &ldquo;improvement&rdquo; by the leading journals;
      and Philip preserved the editorial comments of three or four of them which
      pleased him most. These he used to read aloud to his friends afterwards
      and ask them to guess from which journal each of them had been cut. One
      began in this simple manner:&mdash;

&ldquo;Not a cent, not a dash cent, as I can hear,&rdquo; repeated Mr. Bigler,
      indignantly. &ldquo;I call it a swindle on the state. How it was done gets me. I
      never saw such a tight time for money in Harrisburg.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Not that I knew,&rdquo; said Bigler, shaking his head in disgust. &ldquo;In fact it
      was openly said, that there was no money in the election. It&rsquo;s perfectly
      unheard of.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Perhaps,&rdquo; suggested Philip, &ldquo;it was effected on what the insurance
      companies call the &lsquo;endowment,&rsquo; or the &lsquo;paid up&rsquo; plan, by which a policy
      is secured after a certain time without further payment.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You think then,&rdquo; said Mr. Bolton smiling, &ldquo;that a liberal and sagacious
      politician might own a legislature after a time, and not be bothered with
      keeping up his payments?&rdquo;

&ldquo;We&rsquo;ve got something with money in it,&rdquo; he explained to Mr. Bolton, &ldquo;got
      hold of it by good luck. We&rsquo;ve got the entire contract for Dobson&rsquo;s Patent
      Pavement for the city of Mobile. See here.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Philip tells me,&rdquo; Mrs. Bolton said that evening, &ldquo;that the man Bigler has
      been with thee again to-day. I hope thee will have nothing more to do with
      him.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, mother, his family is in want, and I lent him my name&mdash;but I
      took ample security. The worst that can happen will be a little
      inconvenience.&rdquo;

&ldquo;So, sir,&rdquo; said Ruth, when Philip came from New York, &ldquo;you have been
      assisting in a pretty tragedy. I saw your name in the papers. Is this
      woman a specimen of your western friends?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, she is brilliant; she was called the handsomest woman in Washington&mdash;dashing,
      you know, and sarcastic and witty. Ruth, do you believe a woman ever
      becomes a devil?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I wasn&rsquo;t thinking of that&mdash;it&rsquo;s doubtful if a New York jury would
      find a woman guilty of any such crime. But to think of her life if she is
      acquitted.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, no,&rdquo; said Philip, earnestly, &ldquo;I respect your resolution. But, Ruth,
      do you think you would be happier or do more good in following your
      profession than in having a home of your own?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Nothing, perhaps, only you never would be in it&mdash;you would be away
      day and night, if you had any practice; and what sort of a home would that
      make for your husband?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I can&rsquo;t say we did at first. No, we didn&rsquo;t. Most of the indications were
      there, but not all of them, not all of them. So we thought we&rsquo;d prospect a
      bit.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It was tolerable thick, and looked as if it might be the vein&mdash;looked
      as if it ought to be the vein. Then we went down on it a little. Looked
      better all the time.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, there was the Union, the Alabama and the Black Mohawk&mdash;all
      good, sound mines, you know&mdash;all just exactly like this one when we
      first struck them.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I should be willing to, if it were a necessity, just as I would go
      to war and do what I could, if the country couldn&rsquo;t be saved otherwise,&rdquo;
       said Alice, with a spirit that surprised Philip, well as he thought he
      knew her. &ldquo;If I were a young gentleman in these times&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well,&rdquo; said Philip, looking humble, &ldquo;I care for some things, you and Ruth
      for instance; perhaps I ought not to. Perhaps I ought to care for Congress
      and that sort of thing.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Do you think, Alice,&rdquo; asked Philip with one of those selfish thoughts
      that are not seldom mixed with real love, &ldquo;that Ruth prefers her
      profession to&mdash;to marriage?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Philip,&rdquo; exclaimed Alice, rising to quit the room, and speaking hurriedly
      as if the words were forced from her, &ldquo;you are as blind as a bat; Ruth
      would cut off her right hand for you this minute.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well&mdash;I don&rsquo;t know&mdash;it is a question that requires time; a body
      can&rsquo;t answer every question right off-hand. But it does do good. I am
      satisfied of that.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, that&rsquo;s like last year; and it is a sheer waste of the time for which
      the nation pays those men to work&mdash;that is what that is. And it
      pinches when a body&rsquo;s got a bill waiting.&rdquo;

&ldquo;By that time, the thing has strung out so long that the country is sick
      and tired of it and glad to have a change on any terms. But all that
      inquiry is not lost. It has a good moral effect.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, it&rsquo;s all the same; it&rsquo;s just the same thing. It shows that a man
      can&rsquo;t be corrupt in this country without sweating for it, I can tell you
      that.&rdquo;

&ldquo;But good God we try them, don&rsquo;t we! Is it nothing to show a disposition
      to sift things and bring people to a strict account? I tell you it has its
      effect.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, bother the effect!&mdash;What is it they do do? How do they proceed?
      You know perfectly well&mdash;and it is all bosh, too. Come, now, how do
      they proceed?&rdquo;

&ldquo;It was a terrific thing&mdash;there is no denying that. If he had been
      proven guilty of theft, arson, licentiousness, infanticide, and defiling
      graves, I believe they would have suspended him for two days.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You can depend on it, Washington. Congress is vindictive, Congress is
      savage, sir, when it gets waked up once. It will go to any length to
      vindicate its honor at such a time.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Die and leave the Duchess to fight it out all alone? Oh, no, that won&rsquo;t
      do. Come, now, don&rsquo;t talk so. It is all going to come out right. Now
      you&rsquo;ll see.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It never will, Colonel, never in the world. Something tells me that. I
      get more tired and more despondent every day. I don&rsquo;t see any hope; life
      is only just a trouble. I am so miserable, these days!&rdquo;

&ldquo;I think you are the best soul and the noblest I ever knew, Colonel
      Sellers! and if the people only knew you as I do, you would not be tagging
      around here a nameless man&mdash;you would be in Congress.&rdquo;

Laura stood while the long indictment was read; and at the end, in
      response to the inquiry, of the judge, she said in a clear, low voice;
      &ldquo;Not guilty.&rdquo; She sat down and the court proceeded to impanel a jury.

The man was discharged. &ldquo;He wouldn&rsquo;t have made a bad juror, though,&rdquo;
       whispered Braham. &ldquo;I saw him looking at the prisoner sympathizingly.
      That&rsquo;s a point you want to watch for.&rdquo;

&ldquo;You act like a friend!&rdquo; (sarcastically.) The jury were beginning to hate
      this neatly dressed young sprig. &ldquo;Mister......er....Brierly! Didn&rsquo;t Miss
      Hawkins refuse you?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I object,&rdquo; said Mr. McFlinn; rising to his feet. &ldquo;This has nothing
      whatever to do with the case, your honor. I am surprised at it, even after
      the extraordinary speech of my learned friend.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Of course, your honor. This,&rdquo; continued the Colonel in confidential
      explanation, &ldquo;was twenty years ago. I shouldn&rsquo;t have thought of referring
      to such a trifling circumstance now. If I remember rightly, sir&rdquo;&mdash;

&ldquo;Certainly, your Honor&mdash;certainly. I am not well acquainted with the
      forms of procedure in the courts of New York, but in the West, sir, in the
      West&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;You see, your Honor, there were no questions asked me, and I thought I
      would take advantage of the lull in the proceedings to explain to the jury
      a very significant train of&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Will you state to the jury what has been the effect of the knowledge of
      this wandering and evidently unsettled being, supposed to be her father,
      upon the mind of Miss Hawkins for so many years!&rdquo;

The jury were visibly affected. Half the court room was in tears. If a
      vote of both spectators and jury could have been taken then, the verdict
      would have been, &ldquo;let her go, she has suffered enough.&rdquo;

&ldquo;That&rsquo;s the right talk,&rdquo; said Sellers. &ldquo;That Braham&rsquo;s a wonderful man. He
      was the only man there that really understood me; he told me so himself,
      afterwards.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, well, well, this is trying,&rdquo; said the Colonel. And after a pause,
      &ldquo;No dispatch from Dilworthy for two hours, now. Even a dispatch from him
      would be better than nothing, just to vary this thing.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Come!&rdquo; said Washington. &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t wait for the telegraph boy to come all
      the way up here. Let&rsquo;s go down to Newspaper Row&mdash;meet him on the
      way.&rdquo;

&ldquo;House-Bill&mdash;No. 4,231,&mdash;An-Act-to-Found-and-Incorporate-the
      Knobs-Industrial-University! &mdash;Read-first-and-second-time-considered-in-committee-of-the-whole-ordered-engrossed
      and-passed-to-third-reading-and-final passage!&rdquo;

&ldquo;This is a cruel disappointment,&rdquo; said Mrs. Hawkins, to whom one grief
      more or less did not much matter now, &ldquo;to you and, Washington; but we must
      humbly bear it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I will proceed to business. I am a business man. I am a lecture-agent,
      Miss Hawkins, and as soon as I saw that you were acquitted, it occurred to
      me that an early interview would be mutually beneficial.&rdquo;

&ldquo;No? You see, Miss Hawkins, this is your opportunity. If you will enter
      the lecture field under good auspices, you will carry everything before
      you.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I will think of it, and let you know soon. But still, there is very
      little likelihood that I&mdash;however, we will not discuss it further
      now.&rdquo;

Another Senator said, &ldquo;Let the investigation go on and let it make an
      example of this man Noble; let it teach him and men like him that they
      could not attack the reputation of a United States Senator with impunity.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Why, this is the Senator who franked his baggage home through the mails
      last week&mdash;registered, at that. However, perhaps he was merely
      engaged in &lsquo;upholding the ancient dignity of the Senate,&rsquo;&mdash;then.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Stop, don&rsquo;t sit down on that!&rdquo; exclaimed the Colonel: &ldquo;There, now that&rsquo;s
      all right&mdash;the chair&rsquo;s better. I couldn&rsquo;t get another trunk like that&mdash;not
      another like it in America, I reckon.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Confound it, Washington, you are trying to make fun of me. I don&rsquo;t know
      what has got into you to-day; you act mighty curious. What is the matter
      with you?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I&rsquo;ll tell you, old friend. I am almost happy. I am, indeed. It
      wasn&rsquo;t Clay&rsquo;s telegram that hurried me up so and got me ready to start
      with you. It was a letter from Louise.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Spoken like a man, sir, spoken like a man! Your hand, again my boy! And
      always remember that when a word of advice from Beriah Sellers can help,
      it is at your service. I&rsquo;m going to begin again, too!&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, this is useless, this is absurd. If I found anything it would only be
      one of those little aggravating seams of coal which doesn&rsquo;t mean anything,
      and&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Shust what I said, Mister Sederling,&rdquo; the landlord of the Ilium hotel
      kept repeating. &ldquo;I dold Jake Schmidt he find him dere shust so sure as
      noting.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yaas, I know. But d&rsquo;old woman, she say &lsquo;You sticks to your pisiness. So I
      sticks to &rsquo;em. Und I makes noting. Dat Mister Prierly, he don&rsquo;t never come
      back here no more, ain&rsquo;t it?&rdquo;

Mrs. Bolton led the way to the little chamber where Ruth lay. &ldquo;Oh,&rdquo; said
      her mother, &ldquo;if she were only in her cool and spacious room in our old
      home. She says that seems like heaven.&rdquo;

&ldquo;It is only her indomitable will that has kept her up for weeks. And if
      that should leave her now, there will be no hope. You can do more for her
      now, sir, than I can?&rdquo;
